---
/**
 * TestPlan – the doc testing tool's script for this page. Renders nothing.
 *
 * One block per page, at the end of the content. A plan is ONE NUMBERED LIST and nothing else: a
 * single linear script that walks every testable route the page documents, in order, reverting state
 * between them. Ordering between procedures, and interference between documented alternatives, are
 * handled by the script rather than guessed at by the tool.
 *
 * A plan is the same flow written for a bot, and it MUST NOT DIVERGE FROM THE PAGE ON WHICH CONTROLS
 * IT PRESSES OR WHERE IT PRESSES THEM. A reader and the run go to the same places and press the same
 * buttons, or the run is not evidence about the page. Every run therefore reports any control a plan
 * presses that the page does not name, matched against the page's code-font spans (house style puts
 * UI element names in code font, so those ARE the page's control vocabulary) or, for a multi-word
 * label, a verbatim phrase in its prose. A finding there means the page is missing a step or the plan
 * invented a path; both need a person.
 *
 * A plan is still not graded step-for-step against the prose: `expect rows:` has no counterpart in
 * writing, `go to /path` is positioning rather than a test, and `fork the service` is infrastructure.
 * Only presses are comparable. An earlier rule graded everything and flagged every plan everywhere.
 *
 * Confirmation dialogs are the one press a page is not expected to document — a reader is looking at
 * the dialog and the button says what it does — so they get their own verb, `confirm`, rather than
 * being excused by guesswork. A failing step means something on the page is wrong, and the matching
 * doc step is easy to find by hand.
 *
 * There are no sections. Isolation, Setup, Inputs and Notes were headings once; each was a step
 * wearing a hat, and hiding them made real work invisible to the run and to coverage. Every line is
 * a numbered step, so every line is countable. Text that is not numbered is commentary and ignored.
 *
 * A route the tool can't drive simply gets no steps: absence of steps IS the skip. There is no skip
 * marker, because a declared skip only records what the author remembered to declare and nothing can
 * check that its reason is still true. Absence of the whole block means nobody has written the plan
 * yet, and keeping those two apart is what makes plan coverage countable.
 *
 * A plan is parsed as MDX like the rest of the page, so an angle-bracket placeholder in a step —
 * <database-name> in a documented connection string, say — reads as an unclosed JSX tag and FAILS THE
 * BUILD. Wrap a statement in backticks to make it inline code; the parser strips them back off.
 *
 * Steps use a fixed verb set, so execution stays deterministic and no LLM reads a plan at run time.
 * A line matching no verb is REPORTED, never skipped.
 *
 * Two steps may share a line, joined by ", then": click `Upload your files`, then click `CSV`. The
 * statement verbs (run SQL, run command, replace the statement with) are taken whole instead, so a
 * ", then" inside a statement is never split off.
 *
 * The whole set:
 *
 *   Where the run happens
 *     fork the service              run what follows on a throwaway copy; without this the run uses
 *                                   the standing service and refuses every destructive step
 *     select the service            navigate to the service under test
 *     go to /operations             reach a path directly — positioning and resetting state, never a
 *                                   substitute for a documented click
 *
 *   Files
 *     download https://…            fetch what the page tells the reader to download (archives are
 *                                   unzipped; the folder is psql's cwd and the source for `upload`)
 *     upload sample.csv             put a fetched file into a file input
 *
 *   The interface
 *     click `Label`                 a labelled control
 *     click `A` > `B`               a path through menus or nav, one click per hop
 *     click `X` in `Group`          one option out of several identically-labelled ones
 *     click tab `X`                 name the kind of control (tab, button, link, menuitem) when a
 *                                   label is ambiguous — the Explorer has a `Hypertables` tab AND a
 *                                   sidebar filter button of the same name
 *     toggle `X` on                 a switch; `on`/`off` optional, add "for `row`" to pick a row
 *     confirm `Let's go!`           the button on a confirmation dialog. Fails if no dialog is open,
 *                                   so a plan whose previous step failed to open one stops here
 *                                   instead of pressing something else that matches. Exempt from the
 *                                   control-naming check above; a plain `click` is not
 *     dismiss                       close an open menu or dialog without choosing anything — how a
 *                                   destructive procedure is driven up to its last click and stopped.
 *                                   Fails if nothing is open, for the same reason `confirm` does
 *     type `value` into `Field`     literal text
 *     type $INVITE_EMAIL into `Email`
 *                                   a value from DOCTEST_INPUT_INVITE_EMAIL — never write a real
 *                                   address, CIDR or credential in a plan
 *     use the row for $OWNER_EMAIL  act on the table row holding a value this run owns; "act on the
 *                                   row for" is accepted too
 *     choose an unselected option   where the docs leave the choice open ("choose your strategy")
 *
 *   Data
 *     run SQL: SELECT …             inline; multi-line as indented lines under a trailing ":"
 *     run command: `psql -d "…"`    a shell command; documented placeholders are resolved from the
 *                                   environment at run time
 *     exit psql                     end the psql session, so later SQL goes back through the Console
 *     replace the statement with:   swap the statement a Console editor generated for the one the
 *                                   page gives (the continuous-aggregate wizard). Multi-line as
 *                                   indented lines under the trailing ":", same as run SQL
 *
 *   Assertions
 *     expect rows: <query>          fails when the query returns nothing
 *     expect no rows: <query>       fails when it returns anything
 *     expect url /explorer          fails unless the url contains the fragment — this is what makes
 *                                   a nav step mean "it navigated", not "the label existed"
 *     expect enabled `Leave project`
 *                                   fails unless that control is on the page AND usable. Absent and
 *                                   greyed out are reported differently. Use it for a control a
 *                                   reader is told to press; `expect label` passes on a disabled one
 *     expect label `Meeting policy` fails unless that text is on the page. The assertion of last
 *     expect label $INVITE_EMAIL    resort, for state with no catalog and no url behind it: the
 *                                   high-availability setting, or an invited address in the members
 *                                   table on a page with no SQL at all
 *     expect no label $INVITE_EMAIL fails while that text is STILL on the page — how a removal is
 *                                   asserted where there is no catalog to query for its absence
 *
 *   Escape hatch
 *     [resolve: dropzone]           a control the docs cannot name (a dropzone, a hover-only icon)
 *
 * A plan never names a service or project id. `select the service` means whichever service the run
 * is about, and the id comes from the tool — an id written into a plan rots the next time a standing
 * service is recreated.
 */
---
